Notice that it can be the case that the interval [K^L,K^R) egin{document}$\left [\hat {K}_{L}, \hat {K}_{R}ight)$\end{document} is so narrow that no wavenumber k∈σ(∇2) egin{document}$ oldsymbol{k}\in \sigma \left ({
abla }^{2}ight)$\end{document} satisfies k2∈[K^L,K^R) egin{document}$ oldsymbol{k}^{2}{\in }\left [\hat {K}_{L},\hat {K}_{R}ight)$\end{document}. In this case, the gene network under consideration would not be RD-unstable. However, if the interval is wide enough to admit at least one wavenumber k∈σ(∇2) egin{document}$ oldsymbol{k}\in \sigma \left ({
abla }^{2}ight)$\end{document} such that k2∈[K^L,K^R) egin{document}$ oldsymbol{k}^{2}{\in }\left [\hat {K}_{L},\hat {K}_{R}ight)$\end{document} (i.e. if the gene network is RD-unstable), then there can only be a finite number of such wavenumbers: σ(∇2) egin{document}$\sigma \left ({
abla }^{2}ight)$\end{document} is an unbounded set (Baker et al., 2008) and hence, there will always be some big wavenumber k∈σ(∇2) egin{document}$ oldsymbol{k}\in \sigma \left ({
abla }^{2}ight)$\end{document} for which k2>K^R egin{document}$ oldsymbol{k}^{2} \gt \hat {K}_{R}$\end{document}. This means that, if the gene network is RD-unstable for some choice of model parameters, it can only be RD-unstable of the first kind and thus, the proof of Proposition S2.4 is finished.
